# 2 — Data Analysis

## 2.1 — PCA: Principal Component Analysis

PCA was applied to identify the main dimensions of political variation across Lyon's polling stations. For each election, a matrix of vote shares (stations × blocs) was constructed, centred, and scaled before running PCA via `prcomp()`. Two visualisations are presented in this section: a scree plot computed on a merged matrix of all five elections, and a stacked bar chart comparing the variance explained by PC1 and PC2 across individual elections.

### 2.1.1 Sceen plot

```{r}
#===============================================================================
# Extract numeric matrix (remove bureau column)
X <- all_elections_clean |>
  dplyr::select(-bureau) |>
  as.matrix()

# ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Run PCA
# center = TRUE  :  subtract column mean (centering)
# scale. = TRUE  :  divide by SD (scaling)
#                 
pca <- prcomp(X, center = TRUE, scale. = TRUE)

# Check variance explained by each principal component
importance <- summary(pca)$importance
cat("Variance explained by each PC:\n")
print(round(importance[, 1:6] * 100, 1))  #show first 6

# Plot 1: Scree Plot 

variance_df <- data.frame(
  PC       = paste0("PC", 1:length(pca$sdev)),
  variance = (pca$sdev^2) / sum(pca$sdev^2) * 100,  # convert to %
  cumvar   = cumsum((pca$sdev^2) / sum(pca$sdev^2) * 100)  # cumulative
) |> dplyr::slice(1:6)  #  show first 6


election_pca_plot <- ggplot(variance_df, aes(x = reorder(PC, -variance), y = variance)) +
  geom_col(fill = "#4E79A7", alpha = 0.8) +
  
  geom_text(aes(label = paste0(round(variance, 1), "%")),
            vjust = -0.5, size = 3.2, color = "#4E79A7", fontface = "bold") +
            
  geom_line(aes(group = 1), color = "#E15759", linewidth = 1) +
  geom_point(color = "#E15759", size = 3) +
  geom_line(aes(y = cumvar, group = 1), color = "#76B7B2",
            linewidth = 0.8, linetype = "dashed") +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 80, color = "grey50", linetype = "dotted") +
  annotate("text", x = 6, y = 82, label = "80% cumulative variance",
           size = 3, color = "grey50") +
  labs(
    title    = " Scree Plot —Lyon Elections PCA",
    subtitle = "Blue bars = variance per PC | Red = variance | Dashed = cumulative",
    x        = " Principal Component",
    y        = "Variance Explained (%)"
  ) +
  theme_minimal(base_size = 12) +
  theme(plot.title = element_text(face = "bold"))
  
ggsave("figures/P1_scree_combined.png", plot = election_pca_plot, width = 10, height = 6, dpi = 150)
```

```{r}
knitr::include_graphics("figures/P1_scree_combined.png")
```

The scree plot shows how variance is distributed across principal components in the merged matrix. PC1 explains 40% of total variance. PC2 explains 26%. Together they account for 66%. After PC2, the variance drops sharply. PC3 explains only 8.1%, and the remaining components each contribute less than 5% (PC4: 4.1%, PC5: 2.9%, PC6: 2.3%). The cumulative variance curve shows that around five to six components are needed to reach the 80% threshold. This means PC1 and PC2 capture the two dominant axes of political variation, but they do not represent the majority of total variance in the merged dataset.

### 2.1.2 — PC1+PC2 Variance Explained per Election

```{r}
# Plot 2: Compare PC1 variance explained across elections
# This answers: which election had the most political polarization?
run_pca_single <- function(wide_df, tag) {
  mat <- wide_df |>
    dplyr::select(-bureau) |>
    as.matrix()
  
  pca_res <- prcomp(mat, center = TRUE, scale. = TRUE)
  imp <- summary(pca_res)$importance
  
  list(
    pca    = pca_res,
    bureaux = wide_df$bureau,
    pc1    = round(imp[2, 1] * 100, 1),   # PC1  %
    pc2    = round(imp[2, 2] * 100, 1),   # PC2  %
    tag    = tag
  )
}

pca_eu14    <- run_pca_single(w_eu14,    "EU 2014")
pca_p17r1   <- run_pca_single(w_p17r1,   "Pres 2017 R1")
pca_leg17r1 <- run_pca_single(w_leg17r1, "Leg 2017 R1")
pca_mun20r1 <- run_pca_single(w_mun20r1, "Mun 2020 R1")
pca_p22r1   <- run_pca_single(w_p22r1,   "Pres 2022 R1")

variance_compare <- data.frame(
  election = c("EU 2014", "Pres 2017\nR1", "Leg 2017\nR1",
               "Mun 2020\nR1", "Pres 2022\nR1"),
  pc1 = c(pca_eu14$pc1, pca_p17r1$pc1, pca_leg17r1$pc1,
           pca_mun20r1$pc1, pca_p22r1$pc1),
  pc2 = c(pca_eu14$pc2, pca_p17r1$pc2, pca_leg17r1$pc2,
           pca_mun20r1$pc2, pca_p22r1$pc2)
) |>
  mutate(total = pc1 + pc2,
         election = factor(election, levels = election))

# Convert to long format for stacked bars
variance_long <- variance_compare |>
  pivot_longer(cols = c(pc1, pc2),
               names_to = "component",
               values_to = "variance") |>
  mutate(component = ifelse(component == "pc1", "PC1", "PC2"))

variance_explained <- ggplot(variance_long, aes(x = election, y = variance, fill = component)) +
  geom_col(alpha = 0.85, width = 0.6) +
  
  geom_text(aes(label = paste0(variance, "%")),
            position = position_stack(vjust = 0.5),
            size = 3, color = "white", fontface = "bold") +
            
  geom_text(
    data = variance_compare,
    aes(x = election, y = total + 1.5,
        label = paste0(total, "%")),
    inherit.aes = FALSE, size = 3.5, fontface = "bold"
  ) +
  scale_fill_manual(values = c("PC1" = "#4E79A7", "PC2" = "#A0C4E2"),
                    name = " Component") +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 70, linetype = "dashed", color = "grey50") +
  annotate("text", x = 0.6, y = 71.5,
           label = "70% Reference", size = 3, color = "grey50", hjust = 0) +
  labs(
    title    = " PC1+PC2 Variance Explained per Election",
    subtitle = "Higher = political divide more concentrated on left-right axis",
    x = "", y = " Variance Explained (%)"
  ) +
  theme_minimal(base_size = 12) +
  theme(plot.title = element_text(face = "bold"))

ggsave("figures/P2_variance_per_election.png", plot = variance_explained, width = 10, height = 6, dpi = 150)
```

```{r}
knitr::include_graphics("figures/P2_variance_per_election.png")
```

When PCA is run separately for each election, the combined variance explained by PC1 and PC2 is higher than in the merged matrix. All five elections exceed the 70% reference line. The values range from 70.9% (Leg 2017 R1) to 82.4% (Pres 2022 R1). This shows that within a single election, political variation across polling stations is more structured than the merged result suggests.

The split between PC1 and PC2 also differs across election types. In presidential elections, PC1 is dominant. Pres 2022 R1 has PC1 at 64% and PC2 at only 18.4%. Pres 2017 R1 shows a similar pattern, with PC1 at 54.1% and PC2 at 23.6%. This suggests that in presidential elections, one axis accounts for most of the variation between polling stations. In legislative and municipal elections, the split is more balanced. Leg 2017 R1 has PC1 at 40.2% and PC2 at 30.7%. Mun 2020 R1 has PC1 at 41.5% and PC2 at 33.7%. This indicates that a second dimension of variation plays a larger role in these election types.

The lower total in the merged scree plot reflects the added complexity of combining elections with different political structures into one matrix. It does not indicate a weakness in using two components for individual elections.

### 2.1.3 — Correlation Circles

```{r}

# Plot 3: Correlation circles for all elections (2×3 grid)
# Check if variable directions are consistent across elections

make_circle_data <- function(pca_result, label) {
  as.data.frame(pca_result$pca$rotation[, 1:2]) |>
    rownames_to_column("variable") |>
    mutate(
      election = label,
      length   = sqrt(PC1^2 + PC2^2),
      nuance   = case_when(
        str_detect(variable, "extreme_gauche") ~ "Ext.gauche",
        str_detect(variable, "gauche")         ~ "Gauche",
        str_detect(variable, "centre")         ~ "Centre",
        str_detect(variable, "droite") & !str_detect(variable, "extreme") ~ "Droite",
        str_detect(variable, "extreme_droite") ~ "Ext.droite",
        TRUE ~ "Divers"
      )
    )
}

all_circles <- bind_rows(
  make_circle_data(pca_eu14,    "EU 2014"),
  make_circle_data(pca_p17r1,   "Pres 2017 R1"),
  make_circle_data(pca_leg17r1, "Leg 2017 R1"),
  make_circle_data(pca_mun20r1, "Mun 2020 R1"),
  make_circle_data(pca_p22r1,   "Pres 2022 R1")
) |>
  mutate(election = factor(election,
                           levels = c("EU 2014", "Pres 2017 R1", "Leg 2017 R1",
                                      "Mun 2020 R1", "Pres 2022 R1")))

color_palette <- c(
  "Ext.gauche" = "#B22222",
  "Gauche"     = "#E15759",
  "Centre"     = "#F28E2B",
  "Droite"     = "#4E79A7",
  "Ext.droite" = "#1a1a6e",
  "Divers"     = "#76B7B2"
)

circles_comparison <- ggplot(all_circles, aes(x = PC1, y = PC2, color = nuance)) +
  # Unit circle
  annotate("path",
           x = cos(seq(0, 2*pi, length.out = 100)),
           y = sin(seq(0, 2*pi, length.out = 100)),
           color = "grey80", linetype = "dashed") +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0, color = "grey70") +
  geom_vline(xintercept = 0, color = "grey70") +
  geom_segment(aes(xend = 0, yend = 0),
               arrow = arrow(length = unit(0.15, "cm"), ends = "first"),
               linewidth = 0.7, alpha = 0.8) +
  geom_point(size = 2) +
  geom_text_repel(aes(label = variable), size = 2,
                  max.overlaps = 20, show.legend = FALSE) +
  scale_color_manual(values = color_palette, name = "Nuance") +
  coord_equal(xlim = c(-1.2, 1.2), ylim = c(-1.2, 1.2)) +
  # One panel per election
  facet_wrap(~ election, ncol = 3) +
  labs(
    title    = "Correlation Circles per Election",
    subtitle = " Consistent arrows = stable political structure",
    x = "PC1", y = "PC2"
  ) +
  theme_minimal(base_size = 10) +
  theme(plot.title    = element_text(face = "bold"),
        legend.position = "bottom",
        strip.text    = element_text(face = "bold"))

ggsave("figures/Plot3.png", plot = circles_comparison, width = 20, height = 13, dpi = 150)
```

```{r}
knitr::include_graphics("figures/Plot3.png")
```

The correlation circles show the loadings of each bloc on PC1 and PC2. Across most elections, `gauche` and `droite` point in roughly opposite directions on PC1, consistent with a left–right interpretation of the first axis. Note that PCA loadings are sign-arbitrary, so apparent direction reversals across elections reflect axis flips rather than structural change. A notable pattern appears in Leg 2017 R1 and Mun 2020 R1:

`extreme_gauche` and `extreme_droite` point in similar directions on PC2, while mainstream blocs point in the opposite direction. This suggests that in these elections, PC2 captures an extremes-versus-mainstream axis rather than a secondary left–right distinction. This is consistent with the higher PC2 values observed for these two elections in Figure 2. The position of `centre` varies across elections. In Pres 2022 R1 it is close to `droite`, but in Pres 2017 R1 it occupies a more independent position. The `divers` category, present in EU 2014 only, aggregates candidates not assigned to any main bloc and should be treated as a residual category.

### 2.1.4 — Biplots

```{r}

# ── Biplot───────────────────────────
# PLOT 4/5/6/7— Biplots (one per election, 5 total)

make_biplot <- function(pca_result, wide_df, title, filename) {

  imp     <- summary(pca_result$pca)$importance
  pc1_pct <- round(imp[2, 1] * 100, 1)
  pc2_pct <- round(imp[2, 2] * 100, 1)

  # Station scores
  scores <- as.data.frame(pca_result$pca$x[, 1:2]) |>
    dplyr::mutate(bureau = pca_result$bureaux)

  #  Scale loadings to match score range
  scale_factor <- max(abs(scores[, 1:2])) /
    max(abs(pca_result$pca$rotation[, 1:2])) * 0.65

  loadings <- as.data.frame(pca_result$pca$rotation[, 1:2]) |>
    tibble::rownames_to_column("variable") |>
    dplyr::mutate(
      PC1s   = PC1 * scale_factor,
      PC2s   = PC2 * scale_factor,
      nuance = dplyr::case_when(
        stringr::str_detect(variable, "extreme_gauche") ~ "Ext.gauche",
        stringr::str_detect(variable, "gauche") &
          !stringr::str_detect(variable, "extreme") ~ "Gauche",
        stringr::str_detect(variable, "centre")         ~ "Centre",
        stringr::str_detect(variable, "droite") &
          !stringr::str_detect(variable, "extreme") ~ "Droite",
        stringr::str_detect(variable, "extreme_droite") ~ "Ext.droite",
        TRUE ~ "Divers"
      ),
      length = sqrt(PC1^2 + PC2^2)
    ) |>
    dplyr::group_by(nuance) |>
    dplyr::slice_max(order_by = length, n = 2) |>
    dplyr::ungroup()

  p <- ggplot() +
    geom_hline(yintercept = 0, color = "grey80", linetype = "dashed") +
    geom_vline(xintercept = 0, color = "grey80", linetype = "dashed") +
    # Station points (blue dots)
    geom_point(data  = scores,
               aes(x = PC1, y = PC2),
               alpha = 0.3, color = "steelblue", size = 1.8) +
    #  Extreme station labels
    geom_text_repel(
      data = scores |> dplyr::filter(
        abs(PC1) > quantile(abs(PC1), 0.93) |
        abs(PC2) > quantile(abs(PC2), 0.93)
      ),
      aes(x = PC1, y = PC2, label = bureau),
      size = 2.5, color = "steelblue",
      alpha = 0.8, max.overlaps = 15
    ) +
    # Political nuance arrows
    geom_segment(data = loadings,
                 aes(x = 0, y = 0,
                     xend = PC1s, yend = PC2s,
                     color = nuance),
                 arrow     = arrow(length = unit(0.25, "cm")),
                 linewidth = 1.1, alpha = 0.9) +
    # Arrow labels
    geom_text_repel(data = loadings,
                    aes(x = PC1s, y = PC2s,
                        label = variable, color = nuance),
                    size = 3.2, fontface = "bold",
                    max.overlaps = 30, show.legend = FALSE) +
    scale_color_manual(values = color_palette, name = "Nuance") +
    labs(
      title    = title,
      subtitle = paste0(
        "Blue dots = polling stations | Arrows = political nuances | ",
        "PC1: ", pc1_pct, "% | PC2: ", pc2_pct, "%"
      ),
      x = paste0("PC1 (", pc1_pct, "%)"),
      y = paste0("PC2 (", pc2_pct, "%)")
    ) +
    theme_minimal(base_size = 13) +
    theme(plot.title      = element_text(face = "bold", size = 15),
          legend.position = "right")

  #print(p)
 ggsave(paste0("figures/", filename, ".png"),
        plot = p, width = 11, height = 8, dpi = 150)
  #cat(filename, "\n")
}

#  Generate Biplot for each election
make_biplot(pca_eu14,    w_eu14,
            "Biplot — Européennes 2014",        "biplot_eu14")
make_biplot(pca_p17r1,   w_p17r1,
            "Biplot — Présidentielles 2017 T1", "biplot_pres17")
make_biplot(pca_leg17r1, w_leg17r1,
            "Biplot — Législatives 2017 T1",    "biplot_leg17")
make_biplot(pca_mun20r1, w_mun20r1,
            "Biplot — Municipales 2020 T1",     "biplot_mun20")
make_biplot(pca_p22r1,   w_p22r1,
            "Biplot — Présidentielles 2022 T1", "biplot_pres22")

```

```{r}
knitr::include_graphics("figures/biplot_eu14.png")
```

In EU 2014 (PC1: 52.4%, PC2: 24.1%), `extreme_droite` loads strongly on the negative PC2 axis, pointing in a different direction from all other blocs. This pattern does not appear in any other election.

```{r}
knitr::include_graphics("figures/biplot_pres17.png")
```

In Pres 2017 R1 (PC1: 54.1%, PC2: 23.6%), `centre` points toward the upper-left, away from `droite`. The two blocs occupy clearly separate positions in biplot space.

```{r}
knitr::include_graphics("figures/biplot_leg17.png")
```

In Leg 2017 R1 (PC1: 40.2%, PC2: 30.7%), `extreme_gauche` and `extreme_droite` both point upward along PC2. `gauche` and `droite` are separated along PC1. The station cloud is more spread in both directions than in presidential elections.

```{r}
knitr::include_graphics("figures/biplot_mun20.png")
```

Mun 2020 R1 (PC1: 41.5%, PC2: 33.7%) shows a similar pattern to Leg 2017. Both extreme blocs again point upward along PC2, while `gauche` and `droite` separate along PC1.

```{r}
knitr::include_graphics("figures/biplot_pres22.png")
```

In Pres 2022 R1 (PC1: 64%, PC2: 18.4%), the station cloud is almost entirely spread along the horizontal axis. `centre` and `droite` point in nearly the same direction. The left-right structure is the most concentrated of all five elections.

Across all five elections, stations from the 1st arrondissement (01xx) consistently appear on the gauche side, while 6th arrondissement stations (06xx) consistently appear on the droite side. This suggests that the broad political geography of Lyon's neighbourhoods is stable across election types and years.

## 2.2 — CCA: Canonical Correlation Analysis

CCA was used to measure the extent to which the voting geographies of two elections are linearly related. Before running CCA, the last column of each share matrix was dropped to remove the linear constraint that vote shares sum to 1. The first canonical correlation *r*₁ represents the maximum linear association between a weighted combination of blocs in one election and a weighted combination in the other. Values close to 1 suggest strong geographic stability between the two elections. Lower values suggest more substantial restructuring.

Five comparisons were selected:

1.  Municipal 2020 R1 vs. R2 : within-election round consistency. Note that R2 is included here because CCA only requires the reduced bloc matrix, which remains valid with two rounds even when the number of competing lists differs.
2.  Presidential 2017 R1 vs. Presidential 2022 R1 : stability across two presidential elections
3.  Presidential 2017 R1 vs. Legislative 2017 R1 : stability across election types within the same year
4.  European 2014 vs. Presidential 2017 R1 : cross-year, cross-type comparison
5.  European 2014 vs. Municipal 2020 R1 : a longer temporal horizon comparing two list-based elections

The CCA summary table records *r*₁ and the number of common polling stations for each comparison. The correlation circle plots show, for each election in a pair, how strongly each bloc's vote share correlates with the first two canonical dimensions. Unlike PCA correlation circles, which reflect the internal structure of a single election matrix, these reflect the shared structure between two matrices.

```{r}
#========================
# ============================================================
#  II. CCA — CANONICAL CORRELATION ANALYSIS
# ============================================================

run_cca <- function(m1, m2, tag1, tag2) {
  
  tag <- paste0(tag1, "_vs_", tag2)
  
  # Step 1: find common stations and align row order
  common_ids <- intersect(rownames(m1), rownames(m2))
  m1 <- m1[match(common_ids, rownames(m1)), , drop = FALSE]
  m2 <- m2[match(common_ids, rownames(m2)), , drop = FALSE]
  rownames(m1) <- common_ids
  rownames(m2) <- common_ids
  message("CCA ", tag, ": ", length(common_ids), " common polling stations")

  # Step 2: drop last column to remove linear dependency (shares sum to 1)
  m1_cca <- m1[, -ncol(m1), drop = FALSE]
  m2_cca <- m2[, -ncol(m2), drop = FALSE]

  # Step 3: filter zero-variance and NA/Inf columns for cancor() only
  keep1 <- apply(m1_cca, 2, function(x)
    !any(is.na(x) | is.nan(x) | is.infinite(x)) && var(x) > 0)
  keep2 <- apply(m2_cca, 2, function(x)
    !any(is.na(x) | is.nan(x) | is.infinite(x)) && var(x) > 0)
  m1_cca <- m1_cca[, keep1, drop = FALSE]
  m2_cca <- m2_cca[, keep2, drop = FALSE]

  message("  m1 valid columns (", ncol(m1_cca), "): ",
          paste(colnames(m1_cca), collapse = ", "))
  message("  m2 valid columns (", ncol(m2_cca), "): ",
          paste(colnames(m2_cca), collapse = ", "))

  # Step 4: skip if either matrix has 0 columns
  if (ncol(m1_cca) == 0 || ncol(m2_cca) == 0) {
    warning("CCA ", tag, " skipped: no valid columns after filtering.")
    return(invisible(NULL))
  }

  # Step 5: run CCA
  cca <- stats::cancor(m1_cca, m2_cca)

  # Align coefficient directions for interpretability
  for (d in seq_along(cca$cor)) {
    f <- sign(sum(cca$xcoef[, d] * cca$ycoef[, d]))
    cca$xcoef[, d] <- cca$xcoef[, d] * f
    cca$ycoef[, d] <- cca$ycoef[, d] * f
  }

  # Step 6: compute canonical scores using filtered matrices
  n_can <- min(2, ncol(cca$xcoef), ncol(cca$ycoef))
  U <- m1_cca %*% cca$xcoef[, seq_len(n_can), drop = FALSE]
  V <- m2_cca %*% cca$ycoef[, seq_len(n_can), drop = FALSE]

  # Step 7: correlate full matrices with canonical scores (all blocs shown in plot)
  cor_mat1 <- cor(m1, U)
  colnames(cor_mat1) <- paste0("dim", seq_len(n_can))
  cor_mat2 <- cor(m2, V)
  colnames(cor_mat2) <- paste0("dim", seq_len(n_can))

  # If only one canonical dimension, add dim2 = 0 so ggplot can render
  if (!"dim2" %in% colnames(cor_mat1)) {
    cor_mat1 <- cbind(cor_mat1, dim2 = 0)
    cor_mat2 <- cbind(cor_mat2, dim2 = 0)
  }

  df_plot <- dplyr::bind_rows(
    as.data.frame(cor_mat1) |>
      tibble::rownames_to_column("variable") |>
      dplyr::mutate(election = tag1),
    as.data.frame(cor_mat2) |>
      tibble::rownames_to_column("variable") |>
      dplyr::mutate(election = tag2)
  ) |>
  dplyr::mutate(
    nuance_raw = stringr::str_remove(variable, "^[^_]+_"),
    nuance = dplyr::case_when(
      stringr::str_detect(nuance_raw, "extreme_gauche")                      ~ "Ext.gauche",
      stringr::str_detect(nuance_raw, "gauche") &
        !stringr::str_detect(nuance_raw, "extreme")                          ~ "Gauche",
      stringr::str_detect(nuance_raw, "centre")                              ~ "Centre",
      stringr::str_detect(nuance_raw, "droite") &
        !stringr::str_detect(nuance_raw, "extreme")                          ~ "Droite",
      stringr::str_detect(nuance_raw, "extreme_droite")                      ~ "Ext.droite",
      TRUE                                                                   ~ "Divers"
    )
  )

  circle <- tibble::tibble(
    x = cos(seq(0, 2 * pi, length.out = 300)),
    y = sin(seq(0, 2 * pi, length.out = 300))
  )

  color_palette <- c(
    "Ext.gauche" = "#B22222", "Gauche" = "#E15759",
    "Centre"     = "#F28E2B", "Droite" = "#4E79A7",
    "Ext.droite" = "#1a1a6e", "Divers" = "#76B7B2"
  )

#Plot 9/10/11/12/13: CCA correlation circle plot  
  p <- ggplot2::ggplot(
    df_plot, ggplot2::aes(x = dim1, y = dim2, colour = nuance)
  ) +
    ggplot2::geom_path(
      data = circle,
      ggplot2::aes(x = x, y = y),
      colour = "grey70", inherit.aes = FALSE
    ) +
    ggplot2::geom_hline(yintercept = 0, linetype = "dashed", colour = "grey60") +
    ggplot2::geom_vline(xintercept = 0, linetype = "dashed", colour = "grey60") +
    ggplot2::geom_segment(
      ggplot2::aes(x = 0, y = 0, xend = dim1, yend = dim2),
      arrow = ggplot2::arrow(length = ggplot2::unit(0.15, "cm")),
      linewidth = 0.8
    ) +
    ggrepel::geom_text_repel(
      ggplot2::aes(label = variable),
      size = 3.5, fontface = "bold",
      max.overlaps = 20, show.legend = FALSE
    ) +
    ggplot2::facet_wrap(~ election) +
    ggplot2::scale_color_manual(values = color_palette, name = "Nuance") +
    ggplot2::coord_fixed(xlim = c(-1.05, 1.05), ylim = c(-1.05, 1.05)) +
    ggplot2::labs(
      title    = paste0("CCA — ", tag),
      subtitle = paste0(
        "r1 = ", round(cca$cor[1], 3),
        " | r2 = ", round(cca$cor[2], 3)
      ),
      x = paste0("Canonical dim 1  (r = ", round(cca$cor[1], 3), ")"),
      y = paste0("Canonical dim 2  (r = ", round(cca$cor[2], 3), ")")
    ) +
    ggplot2::theme_minimal(base_size = 12) +
    ggplot2::theme(legend.position = "bottom")

ggsave(paste0("figures/cca_", tag, ".png"), plot = p, width = 10, height = 6, dpi = 150)
  #print(p)

  message("CCA done: ", tag,
          " | r1 = ", round(cca$cor[1], 3),
          " | r2 = ", round(cca$cor[2], 3))

  invisible(list(cca = cca, plot = p, n_stations = length(common_ids)))
}
```

```{r}
# ============================================================
# RUN CCA — 5 comparisons selected for research relevance
# ============================================================

# Round consistency — municipales only (round 2 retains multiple blocs)
cca_mun20_rounds <- run_cca(sm_mun20r1, sm_mun20r2, "mun20r1", "mun20r2")

# Cross-year comparison (round 1 only)
cca_pres_years   <- run_cca(sm_p17r1,   sm_p22r1,   "p17r1",   "p22r1")
cca_p17_leg17    <- run_cca(sm_p17r1,   sm_leg17r1, "p17r1",   "leg17r1")

# Cross-election type (round 1 only)
cca_eu14_p17r1   <- run_cca(sm_eu14,    sm_p17r1,   "eu14",    "p17r1")
cca_eu14_mun20   <- run_cca(sm_eu14,    sm_mun20r1, "eu14",    "mun20r1")
```

### 2.2.1 — Municipal 2020: Round 1 vs. Round 2

```{r}
knitr::include_graphics("figures/cca_mun20r1_vs_mun20r2.png")
```

The first canonical correlation between Mun 2020 R1 and R2 is r1=0.895, indicating strong geographic stability between the two rounds. The second canonical correlation is much lower (r2=0.495), suggesting that the shared structure between rounds is largely captured by a single dimension.
The correlation circles show that in R2, only three blocs are present: `gauche`, `droite`, and `centre`. The `extreme_gauche` and `extreme_droite` arrows disappear, consistent with the consolidation of lists between rounds. Despite this reduction, the directions of gauche, droite, and `centre` remain broadly consistent across the two panels. This confirms that the neighbourhood-level geographic structure of political preferences is preserved between rounds, even as the number of competing lists decreases.

### 2.2.2 — Presidential 2017 R1 vs. Presidential 2022 R1

```{r}
knitr::include_graphics("figures/cca_p17r1_vs_p22r1.png")
```

Comparing the first rounds of two presidential elections five years apart tests the medium-term stability of Lyon's political geography. The candidate landscape changed substantially between the two cycles: the entry of Zemmour as a second far-right candidate in 2022, alongside Le Pen, and the consolidation of the left around Mélenchon, both had the potential to redistribute the geographic pattern of bloc voting. The extent to which the canonical loadings for `centre`, `droite`, and `extreme_droite` shift between the two panels reflects these structural changes. As noted above, the `droite` category became comparatively weaker in 2022, given the fragmentation of the traditional right vote.

### 2.2.3 — Presidential 2017 R1 vs. Legislative 2017 R1

```{r}
knitr::include_graphics("figures/cca_p17r1_vs_leg17r1.png")
```

This comparison tests the stability of voting geography within the same year but across two election types held under different institutional rules. The presidential election is a two-round majority contest for a single national office, which historically encourages tactical voting in round 1; the legislative election is a two-round majority contest in single-member constituencies, where local factors and candidate alliances may play a larger role. The extent to which the geographic profiles of the five blocs are preserved across these two elections reflects how strongly neighbourhood-level preferences are anchored to political tendencies that transcend the specific institutional format.

### 2.2.4 — European 2014 vs. Presidential 2017 R1

```{r}
knitr::include_graphics("figures/cca_eu14_vs_p17r1.png")
```

This comparison introduces a three-year gap and a contrast between a proportional list election (European 2014) and a majoritarian candidate election (Presidential 2017 R1). List-based proportional elections tend to produce higher abstention and attract a more ideologically consistent electorate, while presidential elections mobilise a broader and more heterogeneous one. These structural differences may affect the geographic stability of bloc shares independently of any genuine shifts in political preferences across neighbourhoods, which limits how much of the observed canonical structure can be attributed to temporal change alone.

### 2.2.5 — European 2014 vs. Municipal 2020 R1

```{r}
knitr::include_graphics("figures/cca_eu14_vs_mun20r1.png")
```

This comparison spans the widest temporal gap while comparing two elections that share a broadly similar format — both are list-based, both tend toward higher abstention, and both reduce the incentive for purely tactical voting. The degree of geographic stability observed here, relative to the cross-type comparisons above, may shed some light on the respective contributions of election format and temporal change as drivers of geographic restructuring. That said, the six-year interval also encompasses substantial changes in the national political landscape, which makes it difficult to isolate the effect of election format from that of genuine political shifts.

### 2.2.6 — CCA Summary

```{r}


# Summary table — only include comparisons that were actually run
cca_summary <- tibble::tibble(
  comparison = c(
    "mun20 R1 vs R2",
    "pres 2017 vs 2022",
    "pres 2017 vs leg 2017",
    "eu14 vs pres 2017",
    "eu14 vs mun20"
  ),
  r1 = round(c(
    cca_mun20_rounds$cca$cor[1],
    cca_pres_years$cca$cor[1],
    cca_p17_leg17$cca$cor[1],
    cca_eu14_p17r1$cca$cor[1],
    cca_eu14_mun20$cca$cor[1]
  ), 3),
  n_stations = c(
    cca_mun20_rounds$n_stations,
    cca_pres_years$n_stations,
    cca_p17_leg17$n_stations,
    cca_eu14_p17r1$n_stations,
    cca_eu14_mun20$n_stations
  )
)
print(cca_summary)
```

The summary table collects the first and second canonical correlations for all five comparisons. These values should be interpreted comparatively. Beyond r₁, the second canonical correlation r₂ provides additional information about how much of the secondary geographic structure is preserved. The comparison between Presidential 2017 R1 and Législatives 2017 R1 is notable in that both r₁ (0.956) and r₂ (0.933) are very high, indicating that not only the primary but also the secondary axis of political variation is fully preserved across election types within the same year — the most complete geographic stability observed in the dataset. By contrast, the Municipal 2020 R1 vs R2 comparison shows r₁ = 0.895 but r₂ = 0.495 only, indicating that while the primary geographic axis is preserved between rounds, the secondary structure is substantially reorganised — likely reflecting the consolidation of lists between rounds.

## 2.3 — Clustering

The objective of the clustering analysis is to group Lyon's polling stations into a small number of politically coherent profiles, to characterise the spatial structure of voting behaviour, and to provide a discrete categorical variable for the regression analysis in Section 2.4.

The clustering was performed on a combined feature matrix pooling the vote shares from three elections: Presidential 2017 R1, Municipal 2020 R1, and Presidential 2022 R1. These three elections were selected as the most informative and the most temporally proximate to the 2024 legislative elections used as the regression outcome. The combined matrix thus describes each polling station in terms of its political trajectory across roughly five years.

Two families of methods were applied: k-means clustering and hierarchical clustering. For k-means, the optimal number of clusters was selected via silhouette analysis. For hierarchical clustering, four combinations of distance metric and linkage method were tested, and all solutions were compared using the Adjusted Rand Index (ARI).

### 2.3.1 — Silhouette Analysis and Choice of *k*

```{r}
# IV. Clustering
# ============================================================
# Goal: group polling stations by voting behaviour
# Input: combined share matrix from the most recent elections
# Methods: K-means (multiple k + initializations) +
#          Hierarchical (multiple distances + linkage methods)

# ---- Build combined feature matrix for clustering ----
# Use p17r1, mun20r1, p22r1 — most informative and closest to leg2024
common_bureaux <- Reduce(intersect, list(
  rownames(sm_p17r1),
  rownames(sm_mun20r1),
  rownames(sm_p22r1)
))

X_cluster <- cbind(
  sm_p17r1[common_bureaux, ],
  sm_mun20r1[common_bureaux, ],
  sm_p22r1[common_bureaux, ]
)
cat("Clustering matrix:", nrow(X_cluster), "stations x", ncol(X_cluster), "variables\n")

# PLOT 14 — Silhouette Analysis
sil_scores <- sapply(2:7, function(k) {
  set.seed(42)
  km  <- kmeans(X_cluster, centers = k, nstart = 25)
  sil <- cluster::silhouette(km$cluster, dist(X_cluster))
  mean(sil[, 3])
})

sil_df <- tibble::tibble(k = 2:7, silhouette = sil_scores)
best_k  <- sil_df$k[which.max(sil_df$silhouette)]
cat("Optimal k by silhouette:", best_k, "\n")

silhouette_analysis <- ggplot2::ggplot(sil_df, ggplot2::aes(x = k, y = silhouette)) +
  ggplot2::geom_line(colour = "steelblue", linewidth = 1) +
  ggplot2::geom_point(colour = "steelblue", size = 3) +
  ggplot2::geom_vline(xintercept = best_k,
                      colour = "red", linetype = "dashed") +
  ggplot2::annotate("text", x = best_k + 0.15, y = max(sil_scores) - 0.005,
                    label = paste0("best k = ", best_k),
                    colour = "red", hjust = 0) +
  ggplot2::labs(
    title = "Silhouette analysis — optimal number of clusters",
    x = "Number of clusters k",
    y = "Mean silhouette width (higher = better)"
  ) +
  ggplot2::theme_minimal(base_size = 13)
  
ggsave("figures/silhouette_analysis.png", plot = silhouette_analysis, width = 8, height = 5, dpi = 150)
```

```{r}
knitr::include_graphics("figures/silhouette_analysis.png")
```

The silhouette plot displays the mean silhouette width for *k* ranging from 2 to 7. The mean silhouette width measures, on average, how much more similar each station is to its own cluster than to the nearest alternative cluster: higher values indicate more compact and well-separated clusters. The value of *k* yielding the highest mean silhouette width was selected as the reference solution.

It should be noted that silhouette analysis identifies the most internally coherent partition given the data and the Euclidean distance metric; other criteria might point to a different choice. The selected *k* is therefore used as a working hypothesis rather than a definitive answer, and the sensitivity of subsequent results to this choice is worth bearing in mind.

### 2.3.2 — K-means Clusters on the PCA Plane

```{r}
# K-MEANS CLUSTERING
set.seed(42)
km_k2 <- kmeans(X_cluster, centers = 2,      nstart = 25)
km_k3 <- kmeans(X_cluster, centers = 3,      nstart = 25)
km_k4 <- kmeans(X_cluster, centers = best_k, nstart = 25)
km_k5 <- kmeans(X_cluster, centers = 5,      nstart = 25)

# Compare different random initializations at optimal k
set.seed(1);  km_init1 <- kmeans(X_cluster, centers = best_k, nstart = 1)
set.seed(99); km_init2 <- kmeans(X_cluster, centers = best_k, nstart = 1)
set.seed(42); km_best  <- kmeans(X_cluster, centers = best_k, nstart = 25)

cat("ARI: single init 1 vs best (nstart=25):",
    round(mclust::adjustedRandIndex(km_init1$cluster, km_best$cluster), 3), "\n")
cat("ARI: single init 2 vs best (nstart=25):",
    round(mclust::adjustedRandIndex(km_init2$cluster, km_best$cluster), 3), "\n")
# High ARI = kmeans result is stable regardless of initialization

pca_cluster <- prcomp(X_cluster, center = TRUE, scale. = TRUE)


#PLOT 15/16 — K-means Clusters on PCA Plane (2 plots: k=2, k=3)
plot_kmeans_pca <- function(km_result, pca_res, title) {
  df <- as.data.frame(pca_res$x[, 1:2]) |>
    dplyr::mutate(cluster = factor(km_result$cluster,
                                   labels = paste0("C", seq_len(max(km_result$cluster)))))
  imp <- summary(pca_res)$importance
  ggplot2::ggplot(df, ggplot2::aes(x = PC1, y = PC2, colour = cluster)) +
    ggplot2::geom_point(alpha = 0.45, size = 1.5) +
    ggplot2::stat_ellipse(level = 0.80, linewidth = 0.8) +
    ggplot2::labs(
      title    = title,
      subtitle = "Ellipses show 80% confidence regions per cluster",
      x = paste0("PC1 (", round(imp[2,1]*100, 1), "%)"),
      y = paste0("PC2 (", round(imp[2,2]*100, 1), "%)"),
      colour = "Cluster"
    ) +
    ggplot2::theme_minimal(base_size = 13)
}

# plot_kmeans_pca(km_k2,  pca_cluster, paste0("K-means k=2 on PCA plane"))
# plot_kmeans_pca(km_k3,  pca_cluster, paste0("K-means k=3 on PCA plane"))

ggsave("figures/kmeans_k2_pca.png", plot = plot_kmeans_pca(km_k2, pca_cluster, "K-means k=2 on PCA plane"), width = 8, height = 6, dpi = 150)

ggsave("figures/kmeans_k3_pca.png", plot = plot_kmeans_pca(km_k3, pca_cluster, "K-means k=3 on PCA plane"), width = 8, height = 6, dpi = 150)
```

```{r}
knitr::include_graphics("figures/kmeans_k2_pca.png")
```

```{r}
knitr::include_graphics("figures/kmeans_k3_pca.png")
```

The k-means solutions for *k* = 2 and *k* = 3 are projected onto the first two principal components of the clustering matrix, alongside 80% confidence ellipses for each cluster. These visualisations complement the silhouette analysis by showing whether the clusters occupy distinct regions of the PCA space. The two-cluster solution provides a natural reference: it tends to separate broadly left-leaning stations from broadly right-leaning ones. Whether finer-grained partitions add substantive interpretive value, beyond splitting the two coarse groups, can be assessed by inspecting the ellipses for cluster overlap.

### 2.3.3 — Cluster Political Profiles

```{r}

## PLOT17 — Cluster Political Profile (heatmap)
plot_cluster_profile <- function(km_result, title) {
  BLOCS <- c("extreme_gauche", "gauche", "centre", "droite", "extreme_droite")

  # Average share per cluster, only using p22r1 columns for readability
  cols_p22 <- colnames(X_cluster)[grepl("p22r1", colnames(X_cluster))]

  profile <- as.data.frame(X_cluster[, cols_p22]) |>
    dplyr::mutate(cluster = factor(km_result$cluster,
                                   labels = paste0("Cluster ", seq_len(max(km_result$cluster))))) |>
    dplyr::group_by(cluster) |>
    dplyr::summarise(dplyr::across(dplyr::everything(), mean), .groups = "drop") |>
    tidyr::pivot_longer(-cluster, names_to = "bloc", values_to = "share") |>
    dplyr::mutate(bloc = stringr::str_remove(bloc, "^p22r1_"),
                  bloc = factor(bloc, levels = c(
                    "extreme_gauche", "gauche", "centre",
                    "droite", "extreme_droite")))

  ggplot2::ggplot(profile, ggplot2::aes(x = bloc, y = cluster, fill = share)) +
    ggplot2::geom_tile(colour = "white", linewidth = 0.8) +
    ggplot2::geom_text(ggplot2::aes(label = scales::percent(share, accuracy = 0.1)),
                       size = 3.5) +
    ggplot2::scale_fill_gradient(low = "#f7fbff", high = "#2171b5",
                                  labels = scales::percent_format()) +
    ggplot2::labs(
      title = title,
      x     = "Political bloc (left → right)",
      y     = "Cluster",
      fill  = "Mean share (Pres 2022 R1)"
    ) +
    ggplot2::theme_minimal(base_size = 13) +
    ggplot2::theme(axis.text.x = ggplot2::element_text(angle = 30, hjust = 1))
}

# plot_cluster_profile(km_k4, paste0("Cluster profiles — k=", best_k))

ggsave("figures/cluster_profile_heatmap.png", plot = plot_cluster_profile(km_k4, paste0("Cluster profiles — k=", best_k)), width = 9, height = 6, dpi = 150)
```

```{r}
knitr::include_graphics("figures/cluster_profile_heatmap.png")
```

The heatmap displays the mean vote share per political bloc and per cluster, computed from the Presidential 2022 R1 data. This election was selected for the profile characterisation because it is the most recent in the clustering matrix and provides five well-distributed bloc categories. Each cluster's profile can be read as a stylised description of the type of neighbourhood it captures. Cluster 2 is predominantly left-leaning, with `gauche` averaging 46.6% and `centre` 29.7%; Cluster 1 is more centrist, with `centre` dominating at 40.2% and `gauche` at 28.2%. Notably, `extreme_gauche` shares are similarly low in both clusters (2.0% and 2.9% respectively), indicating that far-left support does not contribute meaningfully to the main axis of differentiation between the two neighbourhood types. These profiles should be interpreted as averages: within-cluster variation is not displayed and may be substantial in some cases.

### 2.3.4 — Hierarchical Clustering: Dendrograms

```{r}
# ---- Hierarchical clustering: compare distances and linkage methods ----
run_hclust <- function(dist_method, link_method, k, tag) {
  d  <- dist(X_cluster, method = dist_method)
  hc <- hclust(d, method = link_method)
  clusters <- cutree(hc, k = k)
  # Plot18/19/20/21 dendrogram
  png(paste0("figures/dendrogram_", tag, ".png"), width = 1000, height = 600)
  plot(hc, labels = FALSE,
       main = paste0("Dendrogram — dist=", dist_method,
                     " | link=", link_method, " | k=", k),
       xlab = "", sub = "")
  rect.hclust(hc, k = k, border = "red")
  dev.off()   # ← 加這一行
  message("HClust done: dist=", dist_method,
          " | link=", link_method, " | k=", k,
          " | tag=", tag)
  invisible(clusters)
}

# Test 4 combinations as required
hc_ward      <- run_hclust("euclidean", "ward.D2",  best_k, "ward")
hc_complete  <- run_hclust("euclidean", "complete", best_k, "complete")
hc_average   <- run_hclust("euclidean", "average",  best_k, "average")
hc_manhattan <- run_hclust("manhattan", "ward.D2",  best_k, "manhattan_ward")
```

```{r}
knitr::include_graphics("figures/dendrogram_ward.png")
```

```{r}
knitr::include_graphics("figures/dendrogram_complete.png")
```

```{r}
knitr::include_graphics("figures/dendrogram_average.png")
```

```{r}
knitr::include_graphics("figures/dendrogram_manhattan_ward.png")
```

Four hierarchical clustering solutions were computed: Euclidean distance with Ward, complete, and average linkage, and Manhattan distance with Ward linkage. The dendrograms illustrate the hierarchical merging process; the red lines mark the partition at *k* clusters. Ward linkage with Euclidean distance tends to produce more compact and roughly equal-sized clusters; complete linkage produces more balanced but sometimes looser clusters; average linkage produces a severely degenerate partition in this dataset due to chaining: almost all polling stations are absorbed into a single cluster, rendering this solution uninformative and excluding it from substantive comparison. Manhattan distance provides a degree of robustness to outliers in high-dimensional spaces. Three of the four methods — Ward, complete, and Manhattan+Ward linkage — produce broadly consistent two-cluster solutions, providing reassurance that the k=2 partition is not an artefact of a particular algorithm. Average linkage produces a severely degenerate partition due to chaining, with almost all stations collapsed into a single cluster; this solution is uninformative and excluded from substantive comparison

### 2.3.5 — ARI Comparison Across Methods

```{r, echo=FALSE}
ari_results <- tibble::tibble(
  comparison = c("kmeans vs hclust ward", "kmeans vs hclust complete",
                 "kmeans vs hclust average", "kmeans vs hclust manhattan+ward",
                 "hclust ward vs complete", "hclust ward vs average",
                 "hclust ward vs manhattan+ward"),
  ARI = round(c(
    mclust::adjustedRandIndex(km_k4$cluster, hc_ward),
    mclust::adjustedRandIndex(km_k4$cluster, hc_complete),
    mclust::adjustedRandIndex(km_k4$cluster, hc_average),
    mclust::adjustedRandIndex(km_k4$cluster, hc_manhattan),
    mclust::adjustedRandIndex(hc_ward,       hc_complete),
    mclust::adjustedRandIndex(hc_ward,       hc_average),
    mclust::adjustedRandIndex(hc_ward,       hc_manhattan)
  ), 3),
  interpretation = dplyr::case_when(
    ARI > 0.8 ~ "Very similar",
    ARI > 0.6 ~ "Similar",
    ARI > 0.4 ~ "Moderate",
    TRUE      ~ "Weak"
  )
)
print(ari_results)
```

The ARI table summarises the pairwise agreement between all clustering methods. An ARI close to 1 indicates near-identical partitions; an ARI near 0 indicates agreement no better than chance. High agreement between k-means and several hierarchical solutions would provide reassurance that the identified clusters are not artefacts of a particular algorithm. Conversely, low agreement, particularly for average linkage — would suggest that the partition is sensitive to algorithmic choices, and that substantive interpretations should be made with appropriate caution.

## 2.4 — Regression: Predicting the 2024 Legislative Elections

The regression analysis uses the political geography characterised in the preceding sections to predict the first-round results of the 2024 legislative elections in Lyon's polling stations. This constitutes the main predictive objective of the project.

### 2.4.1 — Data preparation

Two additional datasets were acquired for this section: the European 2024 results and the Legislative 2024 R1 results, both filtered to Lyon (commune code 69123) from CSV files published by the French Ministry of the Interior. The nuance codes in these datasets follow the official classification system used in the earlier elections and were mapped to the same five-bloc scheme. The European 2024 results serve as an additional explanatory variable; the Legislative 2024 R1 results are the response variable.

The regression dataset was constructed by joining all wide share tables on polling station identifier (`bureau`). The cluster label from the k=2 k-means solution (Section 2.3) was joined as a two-level categorical variable (C1 and C2), with C1 serving as the reference category. This variable provides a discrete summary of each station's historical political profile — left-leaning (C2) versus centrist (C1).

### 2.4.2 — Model specification

One linear regression model was estimated per political bloc:

$$\hat{y}_{\text{leg2024},b} = \beta_0 + \beta_1 x_{\text{eu14},b} + \beta_2 x_{\text{p17r1},b} + \beta_3 x_{\text{leg17r1},b} + \beta_4 x_{\text{mun20r1},b} + \beta_5 x_{\text{p22r1},b} + \beta_6 x_{\text{euro2024},b} + \gamma_k + \varepsilon$$

where $b$ denotes the political bloc, $x_{e,b}$ is the vote share for bloc $b$ in election $e$, and $\gamma_k$ is a fixed effect for the cluster $k$ to which the station belongs. The cluster fixed effect captures systematic differences in baseline vote levels across neighbourhood types that are not explained by the bloc-specific temporal predictors alone.

It should be noted that this specification treats each bloc independently: vote shares across blocs within a station are not constrained to sum to 1 in the regression. This simplification allows each model to be estimated and interpreted separately, but may introduce some inconsistency if predictions are aggregated.

```{r}
#
# ============================================================
# V. REGRESSION — Download, clean, and prepare 2024 data
# ============================================================

# Download
files_2024 <- list(
  # Parlement européen, 2014
  list(
    url = "https://www.data.gouv.fr/api/1/datasets/r/cc1883d9-1265-4365-b754-fb6aef22d82e",
    dest = "euro2024.csv"
  ),
  
  # Présidentielles, 2017, 1st round
  list(
    url = "https://www.data.gouv.fr/api/1/datasets/r/6813fb28-7ec0-42ff-a528-2bc3d82d7dcd",
    dest = "leg2024_r1.csv"
  )
)

for (f in files_2024) {
  download_if_not_local(f$url, f$dest)
}

# ---- Load new datasets ----
euro2024_raw   <- read.csv("data_raw/euro2024.csv",   sep = ";", encoding = "UTF-8")
leg2024_r1_raw <- read.csv("data_raw/leg2024_r1.csv", sep = ";", encoding = "UTF-8")

# ---- Filter Lyon only (code commune = 69123) ----
euro2024_lyon    <- euro2024_raw   |> dplyr::filter(Code.commune == 69123)
leg2024_r1_lyon  <- leg2024_r1_raw |> dplyr::filter(Code.commune == 69123)

cat("Euro 2024 Lyon bureaux:", nrow(euro2024_lyon), "\n")
cat("Leg 2024 R1 Lyon bureaux:", nrow(leg2024_r1_lyon), "\n")

# ---- Inspect nuance codes ----
# Euro 2024: nuances are in Nuance.liste.1, Nuance.liste.2, ...
euro_nuances <- euro2024_lyon |>
  dplyr::select(dplyr::matches("^Nuance\\.liste\\.")) |>
  unlist() |> unique() |> sort()
cat("Euro 2024 nuances:\n"); print(euro_nuances)

# Leg 2024: nuances are in Nuance.candidat.1, Nuance.candidat.2, ...
leg_nuances <- leg2024_r1_lyon |>
  dplyr::select(dplyr::matches("^Nuance\\.candidat\\.")) |>
  unlist() |> unique() |> sort()
cat("Leg 2024 R1 nuances:\n"); print(leg_nuances)


# ---- pivot_wider into long ----

# Euro 2024: pivot Nuance.liste.N + Voix.N → long format
euro2024_long <- euro2024_lyon |>
  dplyr::select(Code.BV,
                dplyr::matches("^Nuance\\.liste\\.\\d+$"),
                dplyr::matches("^Voix\\.\\d+$")) |>
  tidyr::pivot_longer(
    cols      = -Code.BV,
    names_to  = c(".value", "num"),
    names_pattern = "^(Nuance\\.liste|Voix)\\.(\\d+)$"
  ) |>
  dplyr::filter(!is.na(Nuance.liste), Voix > 0) |>
  dplyr::mutate(
    bureau = stringr::str_pad(as.character(Code.BV), 4, "left", "0"),
    nuance = Nuance.liste,
    votes  = Voix
  ) |>
  dplyr::select(bureau, nuance, votes)

# Leg 2024 R1: same structure but column names differ
leg2024_r1_long <- leg2024_r1_lyon |>
  dplyr::select(Code.BV,
                dplyr::matches("^Nuance\\.candidat\\.\\d+$"),
                dplyr::matches("^Voix\\.\\d+$")) |>
  tidyr::pivot_longer(
    cols      = -Code.BV,
    names_to  = c(".value", "num"),
    names_pattern = "^(Nuance\\.candidat|Voix)\\.(\\d+)$"
  ) |>
  dplyr::filter(!is.na(Nuance.candidat), Voix > 0) |>
  dplyr::mutate(
    bureau = stringr::str_pad(as.character(Code.BV), 4, "left", "0"),
    nuance = Nuance.candidat,
    votes  = Voix
  ) |>
  dplyr::select(bureau, nuance, votes)

# ---- Nuance mapping for Euro 2024 ----
# Source: https://www.resultats-elections.interieur.gouv.fr
nuances_mapping_euro2024 <- list(
  extreme_gauche = list("LEXG", "LFI", "LCOM"),
  gauche         = list("LDVG", "LUG", "LVEC", "LECO"),
  centre         = list("LENS", "LREC"),
  droite         = list("LDVD", "LLR"),
  extreme_droite = list("LEXD", "LRN"),
  divers         = list("LDIV")
)

# ---- Nuance mapping for Leg 2024 R1 ----
nuances_mapping_leg2024 <- list(
  extreme_gauche = list("EXG"),
  gauche         = list("UG", "ECO"),
  centre         = list("ENS", "UDI", "REC"),
  droite         = list("LR"),
  extreme_droite = list("RN"),
  divers         = list("DIV")
)

# ---- Pivot Euro 2024 to long format ----
euro2024_long <- euro2024_lyon |>
  dplyr::select(Code.BV,
                dplyr::matches("^Nuance\\.liste\\.\\d+$"),
                dplyr::matches("^Voix\\.\\d+$")) |>
  tidyr::pivot_longer(
    cols         = -Code.BV,
    names_to     = c(".value", "num"),
    names_pattern = "^(Nuance\\.liste|Voix)\\.(\\d+)$"
  ) |>
  dplyr::filter(!is.na(Nuance.liste), Voix > 0) |>
  dplyr::mutate(
    bureau = stringr::str_pad(as.character(Code.BV), 4, "left", "0"),
    nuance = Nuance.liste,
    votes  = Voix
  ) |>
  dplyr::select(bureau, nuance, votes) |>
  replace_nuance(nuances_mapping_euro2024)

# ---- Pivot Leg 2024 R1 to long format ----
leg2024_r1_long <- leg2024_r1_lyon |>
  dplyr::select(Code.BV,
                dplyr::matches("^Nuance\\.candidat\\.\\d+$"),
                dplyr::matches("^Voix\\.\\d+$")) |>
  tidyr::pivot_longer(
    cols         = -Code.BV,
    names_to     = c(".value", "num"),
    names_pattern = "^(Nuance\\.candidat|Voix)\\.(\\d+)$"
  ) |>
  dplyr::filter(!is.na(Nuance.candidat), Nuance.candidat != "", Voix > 0) |>
  dplyr::mutate(
    bureau = stringr::str_pad(as.character(Code.BV), 4, "left", "0"),
    nuance = Nuance.candidat,
    votes  = Voix
  ) |>
  dplyr::select(bureau, nuance, votes) |>
  replace_nuance(nuances_mapping_leg2024)

# ---- Convert to wide share tables ----
w_euro2024   <- make_wide(euro2024_long,   "euro2024")
w_leg2024_r1 <- make_wide(leg2024_r1_long, "leg2024")

w_euro2024   <- ensure_nuances(w_euro2024,   "euro2024")
w_leg2024_r1 <- ensure_nuances(w_leg2024_r1, "leg2024")

cat("Euro 2024 bureaux:", nrow(w_euro2024), "\n")
cat("Leg 2024 R1 bureaux:", nrow(w_leg2024_r1), "\n")

# ---- Build regression dataset ----
# Join all explanatory variables + response variable on bureau ID
# Use only round 1 elections as explanatory variables
reg_data <- w_leg2024_r1 |>
  dplyr::inner_join(w_eu14,    by = "bureau") |>
  dplyr::inner_join(w_p17r1,   by = "bureau") |>
  dplyr::inner_join(w_leg17r1, by = "bureau") |>
  dplyr::inner_join(w_mun20r1, by = "bureau") |>
  dplyr::inner_join(w_p22r1,   by = "bureau") |>
  dplyr::inner_join(w_euro2024, by = "bureau") |>
  # Add cluster label from k-means as a categorical explanatory variable
  dplyr::left_join(
    tibble::tibble(
      bureau  = names(km_k4$cluster),
      cluster = factor(km_k4$cluster, labels = paste0("C", seq_len(max(km_k4$cluster))))
    ),
    by = "bureau"
  ) |>
  na.omit()

cat("Regression dataset:", nrow(reg_data), "polling stations\n")
cat("Variables:", ncol(reg_data), "\n")

# ============================================================
# Run one linear regression per political bloc
# Y = leg2024 bloc share
# X = same bloc share across all past elections + cluster
# ============================================================

blocs <- c("extreme_gauche", "gauche", "centre", "droite", "extreme_droite")

run_bloc_regression <- function(bloc, data) {

  y_col <- paste0("leg2024_", bloc)

  # Explanatory variables: same bloc from each past election + cluster
  x_cols <- c(
    paste0("eu14_",     bloc),
    paste0("p17r1_",    bloc),
    paste0("leg17r1_",  bloc),
    paste0("mun20r1_",  bloc),
    paste0("p22r1_",    bloc),
    paste0("euro2024_", bloc),
    "cluster"
  )

  # Keep only columns that exist in the data
  x_cols <- x_cols[x_cols %in% colnames(data)]

  formula <- as.formula(paste(y_col, "~", paste(x_cols, collapse = " + ")))
  model   <- lm(formula, data = data)

  list(bloc = bloc, model = model, summary = summary(model))
}

# Run all 5 models
reg_models <- lapply(blocs, run_bloc_regression, data = reg_data)
names(reg_models) <- blocs

```

### 2.4.3 — R² per bloc

```{r}
# ============================================================
# PLOT: Regression results summary
# R² per bloc + coefficient plot
# ============================================================

# ---- Table: R² and key statistics per bloc ----
reg_summary <- tibble::tibble(
  bloc    = blocs,
  r2      = sapply(reg_models, function(m) round(summary(m$model)$r.squared, 3)),
  adj_r2  = sapply(reg_models, function(m) round(summary(m$model)$adj.r.squared, 3)),
  p_value = sapply(reg_models, function(m) {
    f <- summary(m$model)$fstatistic
    round(pf(f[1], f[2], f[3], lower.tail = FALSE), 4)
  })
) |>
  dplyr::mutate(
    fit_quality = dplyr::case_when(
      r2 > 0.8 ~ "Excellent",
      r2 > 0.6 ~ "Good",
      r2 > 0.4 ~ "Moderate",
      TRUE     ~ "Weak"
    )
  )
```

```{r}
# ---- Plot 1: R² comparison across blocs ----
regression_r2_plot <- ggplot2::ggplot(reg_summary,
                ggplot2::aes(x = reorder(bloc, r2), y = r2, fill = fit_quality)) +
  ggplot2::geom_col(width = 0.6, alpha = 0.85) +
  ggplot2::geom_text(ggplot2::aes(label = paste0("R²=", r2)),
                     hjust = -0.1, size = 3.5, fontface = "bold") +
  ggplot2::scale_fill_manual(
  values = c("Excellent" = "#2E5F8A", "Good"     = "#5B8DB8",
             "Moderate"  = "#A0BDD4", "Weak"     = "#6B7F8E"),
  name = "Fit quality"
  ) +
  ggplot2::coord_flip(ylim = c(0, 1.05)) +
  ggplot2::labs(
    title    = "Regression R² per Political Bloc",
    subtitle = "Response: Leg 2024 R1 | Explanatory: elections 2014–2024 + cluster",
    x        = "Political bloc",
    y        = "R² (proportion of variance explained)"
  ) +
  theme_minimal(base_size = 12) +
theme(plot.title       = element_text(face = "bold"),
      plot.subtitle    = element_text(colour = "grey40"),
      legend.position  = "right",
      panel.grid.minor = element_blank())

ggsave("figures/regression_r2.png", plot = regression_r2_plot, width = 9, height = 5, dpi = 150)
```

```{r}
knitr::include_graphics("figures/regression_r2.png")
```

The bar chart displays the R² for each of the five bloc-specific models. In general, blocs whose vote share is geographically stable across elections, meaning that the same areas tend to vote consistently for the same bloc which are expected to yield higher R² values. Blocs that experienced more substantial geographic restructuring between 2022 and 2024 are expected to yield lower values. `Gauche` achieves the best fit (R² = 0.966), followed by `extreme_droite` (0.935), `centre` (0.874), and `droite` (0.829). `Extreme_gauche` is a clear outlier at R² = 0.437, the only bloc for which the model fit is classified as moderate rather than excellent, reflecting the greater volatility of far-left geographic support across the study period.

These figures should be interpreted cautiously. A high R² indicates good in-sample fit but does not guarantee out-of-sample accuracy; the ratio of predictors to observations means that some degree of over-fitting is plausible, and adjusted R² values — also reported — provide a partial correction.

### 2.4.4 — Coefficient plots

```{r}
# ---- Plot 2: Coefficient plot for each bloc ----
# 

plot_coefficients <- function(reg_model_item) {
  coef_df <- broom::tidy(reg_model_item$model, conf.int = TRUE) |>
    dplyr::filter(term != "(Intercept)") |>
    dplyr::mutate(
      significant = p.value < 0.05,
      term        = stringr::str_remove(term, "cluster")
    )
  p <- ggplot2::ggplot(coef_df,
                  ggplot2::aes(x = reorder(term, estimate),
                               y = estimate,
                               colour = significant)) +
    ggplot2::geom_hline(yintercept = 0, linetype = "dashed", colour = "grey60") +
    ggplot2::geom_pointrange(
      ggplot2::aes(ymin = conf.low, ymax = conf.high),
      linewidth = 0.8, size = 0.6
    ) +
    ggplot2::scale_colour_manual(
      values = c("TRUE" = "#4E79A7", "FALSE" = "grey70"),
      labels = c("TRUE" = "p < 0.05", "FALSE" = "p ≥ 0.05"),
      name   = "Significance"
    ) +
    ggplot2::coord_flip() +
    ggplot2::labs(
      title    = paste0("Coefficients — ", reg_model_item$bloc),
      subtitle = paste0("R² = ", round(summary(reg_model_item$model)$r.squared, 3)),
      x        = "",
      y        = "Coefficient (95% CI)"
    ) +
    ggplot2::theme_minimal(base_size = 12) +
    ggplot2::theme(plot.title       = ggplot2::element_text(face = "bold"),
                   plot.subtitle    = ggplot2::element_text(colour = "grey40"),
                   legend.position  = "right",
                   panel.grid.minor = ggplot2::element_blank())
  ggsave(paste0("figures/coef_", reg_model_item$bloc, ".png"),
         plot = p, width = 9, height = 6, dpi = 150)
  return(p)
}


# Install broom if needed
if (!requireNamespace("broom", quietly = TRUE)) install.packages("broom")
library(broom)

# Generate one coefficient plot per bloc
lapply(reg_models, plot_coefficients) |> invisible()

```

For each bloc, the coefficient plot shows the estimated regression coefficients and their 95% confidence intervals, with statistically significant coefficients (p \< 0.05) highlighted in blue. The most recent elections carry the largest predictive weight for most blocs, but the pattern varies substantially across blocs.

```{r}
knitr::include_graphics("figures/coef_gauche.png")
```

```{r}
knitr::include_graphics("figures/coef_centre.png")
```

```{r}
knitr::include_graphics("figures/coef_extreme_droite.png")
```

For gauche, centre, and extreme_droite, Presidential 2022 R1 is the dominant predictor with a large and statistically significant coefficient; European 2024 adds a smaller but significant contribution.

```{r}
knitr::include_graphics("figures/coef_droite.png")
```

For droite, by contrast, European 2024 is the dominant predictor while Presidential 2022 R1 is not statistically significant, suggesting that the traditional right's geographic support underwent substantial restructuring between 2022 and 2024.

```{r}
knitr::include_graphics("figures/coef_extreme_gauche.png")
```

For extreme_gauche, the strongest predictor is Presidential 2017 R1, and the coefficient on Législatives 2017 R1 is negative and significant, indicating that far-left geographic support changed direction inconsistently across elections, which is consistent with the low R² of 0.437 for this bloc. Earlier elections may have coefficients not statistically distinguishable from zero for most blocs, reflecting genuine geographic restructuring over the study period.

### 2.4.5 — Summary table and limitations

```{r}

# print(reg_summary)

reg_summary

# # ---- Print full model summaries ----
# for (bloc in blocs) {
#   cat("\n", rep("=", 60), "\n")
#   cat("MODEL:", bloc, "\n")
#   cat(rep("=", 60), "\n")
#   print(summary(reg_models[[bloc]]$model))
# }
```

The regression analysis provides a data-driven characterisation of the geographic predictability of Lyon's 2024 legislative results. Several limitations should be borne in mind. First, the five-bloc classification scheme aggregates candidates with heterogeneous profiles and may obscure important within-bloc variation, particularly for blocs that fielded coalition lists in 2024. Second, the model does not account for changes in which candidates stood in each polling station's legislative constituency between elections, which can affect bloc shares independently of any genuine shift in voter preferences. Third, only polling-station-level electoral data are used; the inclusion of sociodemographic variables would likely enrich the predictive model. Finally, the results are specific to the city of Lyon and should not be generalised to other French cities without further investigation.

